{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_time",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The type of incident (email, database, vpn) is a common cause of both how it is triaged/routed (treatment) and how long it takes to resolve. Not an intervenable lever itself; adjusting for it is needed. Note the original table already has 'category', so this partly restates named context."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_system",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The affected system influences both assignment/handling and inherent resolution difficulty; a common cause, not a settable treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_symptom",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The observed symptom reflects intrinsic incident difficulty affecting resolution time and how it is handled; a pre-treatment common cause, not intervenable."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_unassigned",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an incident is left unassigned is an operational, settable lever an actor can change (assign it or not), plausibly affecting resolution time."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolver_match",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the resolver matches the appropriate group/skill is an assignment decision that can be set, making it an intervenable treatment on resolution time."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_open_unresolved",
      "causal_role": "outcome",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Being open/unresolved is essentially a restatement of the outcome status (resolution not yet achieved); it is downstream of/definitionally tied to resolution time, not a treatment or confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_segment",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Caller type (end_user vs automated) can influence both routing/handling and resolution duration; a pre-treatment common cause rather than a settable lever."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["is_unassigned", "resolver_match"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "incident_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "affected_system", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "failure_symptom", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.65},
    {"name": "caller_segment", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.5}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The added columns include genuinely intervenable treatments: 'is_unassigned' and 'resolver_match' are assignment decisions an operator can set, directly serving a what-if question about reducing resolution time. Several confounders (incident_category, affected_system, failure_symptom, caller_segment) capture intrinsic incident difficulty and caller type that are common causes of both how an incident is assigned/handled and how long it takes to resolve, and none are named in the query (though 'category'/'assignment_group' exist in the base table, the added semantic-difficulty columns go beyond named context). The set weakens somewhat because 'is_open_unresolved' is essentially a restatement of the outcome rather than a distinct causal variable, and the confounders overlap with fields already present. Overall the intervention framing is satisfied with a moderate-quality confounder set."
}

